BareProxy load experiments: what the 20 applies of the load test stay clear of, 5 October 2026

live/load-experiments.sh runs five cases under load, each on its own BareProxy with two test backends, 1,000 requests per second
over HTTP/1.1 for 24 seconds (the WebSocket case: 400 messages per second on 20 connections for 20 seconds). The applies come
every 1.4 seconds from the 4th second. None of this is part of the load test's pass rule. This run is on the final code
(tree fff3e3b, 16:50, nothing else running on the machine). The first run, on the code before the fix of commit 9965f84,
is in the 'before' column.

case     what each apply does                                      applies    sent  failed  before  how it fails now
drain    removes a backend from the pool with the WebSocket tunnels      1   8,020      10      11  the tunnels through it close when the 3 s drain ends
newpool  adds a new pool and points a route at it, in one apply        10  24,000       0      25  none
twostep  adds the new pool, then 0.6 s later points the route at it    20  24,000       0       0  none
health   changes a pool's health line                                  10  24,000       0      10  none
swap     replaces the only backend of a pool with another one          10  24,000       0      10  none

What they show
  newpool, health and swap: an apply swaps in the new config before the first health check of its new backends has finished.
  Before the fix, a backend that is new to its pool (a new pool, a pool whose health line changed, which counts as new backends,
  or a replaced backend) took no requests until that check passed, so a route with no other backend answered 503 for a
  millisecond or two: 1 to 2.5 failed requests per apply at 1,000 requests per second (computed from the 'before' counts).
  Since 9965f84, a backend still waiting for its first check takes requests when no backend in its pool is up, and the three
  cases lose nothing. While another backend in the pool is up, a new one still waits for its check.
  drain: a WebSocket connection is a tunnel, and when the pool's drain time ends BareProxy closes the connections of the removed
  backend, in use or not. That is what the README says about drain (connections close after the drain time, 30 seconds by
  default), and it means every apply that removes a backend cuts the WebSocket connections that went through it once the drain
  time is over. The load test's applies leave the WebSocket pool alone for that reason.

